1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,823
The brush uncovers a clay seal, round, with writing around the edge.

2
00:00:04,823 --> 00:00:11,888
Seals belong to institutions — a seal exists so that a document can be authenticated as coming from a particular body.

3
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:19,700
An institution with its own seal is an institution that outlives the people in it.

4
00:00:22,733 --> 00:00:29,849
Travellers above all, because two Chinese pilgrims lived and studied at Nalanda and described it from the inside.

5
00:00:29,849 --> 00:00:31,347
Inscriptions and seals.

6
00:00:31,347 --> 00:00:35,841
Excavation, which has uncovered the monastery blocks and lecture halls.

7
00:00:35,841 --> 00:00:39,074
And texts produced by the scholars themselves.

8
00:00:41,333 --> 00:00:43,489
Four to know, in rough order.

9
00:00:43,489 --> 00:00:51,979
Taxila, in the north-west, famous from about the sixth century BCE. Nalanda, in Bihar, from the fifth century CE.

10
00:00:55,233 --> 00:01:00,458
Vallabhi, in Gujarat, a centre of a different Buddhist school and of secular subjects.

11
00:01:00,458 --> 00:01:06,462
And Vikramashila, in Bihar, founded by the Palas, with a strong reputation in tantric studies.

12
00:01:08,833 --> 00:01:14,551
Taxila and Nalanda were not the same kind of place, and this is where questions catch people out.

13
00:01:14,551 --> 00:01:20,734
Taxila, as the sources describe it, is a city where distinguished teachers each took students — medicine,

14
00:01:20,734 --> 00:01:27,743
archery, law, the Vedas — with students attaching themselves to a teacher rather than enrolling in an institution.

15
00:01:32,700 --> 00:01:38,503
Nalanda is a single endowed complex with buildings, a curriculum, residence and a governing body.

16
00:01:38,503 --> 00:01:42,355
The second is much closer to what we now call a university.

17
00:01:47,900 --> 00:01:49,881
Nalanda's plan is still visible.

18
00:01:49,881 --> 00:01:54,684
Monastery blocks in a row along one side, temples facing them across an avenue,

19
00:01:54,684 --> 00:01:58,706
each block built around a courtyard with cells for residents.

20
00:02:02,033 --> 00:02:05,129
It was endowed by Gupta rulers and later by the Palas,

21
00:02:05,129 --> 00:02:11,263
and by donations from as far away as Southeast Asia — one copper plate records a grant from a Sumatran king.

22
00:02:11,263 --> 00:02:16,402
Its library was large enough that later accounts describe it burning for a long time.

23
00:02:20,433 --> 00:02:26,230
Hiuen Tsang studied there in the seventh century and describes an entrance examination at the gate,

24
00:02:26,230 --> 00:02:29,476
conducted by a scholar who turned away most applicants.

25
00:02:29,476 --> 00:02:33,775
I-Tsing, later, describes the daily routine and the rules in detail.

26
00:02:38,433 --> 00:02:43,449
Subjects went well beyond Buddhist doctrine: logic, grammar, medicine, mathematics.

27
00:02:43,449 --> 00:02:50,434
Nalanda was destroyed in the late twelfth or early thirteenth century during the Turkish invasions of eastern India,

28
00:02:50,434 --> 00:02:52,167
and it did not recover.

29
00:02:55,133 --> 00:02:56,560
Was Taxila a university?

30
00:02:56,560 --> 00:03:03,596
One position says yes in substance — it drew students from great distances and taught at the highest level available.

31
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:13,438
The other says the word misleads: there is no evidence of a single institution, a common curriculum,

32
00:03:13,438 --> 00:03:19,911
enrolment or a governing body, and applying the term imports a European institutional idea invented much later.

33
00:03:19,911 --> 00:03:26,395
The safest answer describes what the sources actually say and notes that Nalanda fits the term far better.

34
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:36,239
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

35
00:03:36,239 --> 00:03:41,132
Taxila, Nalanda, Vallabhi, Vikramashila — oldest to latest.

36
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:47,673
Dig Quiz.

37
00:03:47,673 --> 00:03:48,952
Pause and name it.

38
00:03:48,952 --> 00:03:56,387
First: the seventh-century Chinese pilgrim who studied at Nalanda and described its entrance examination.

39
00:04:00,767 --> 00:04:01,701
Hiuen Tsang.

40
00:04:01,701 --> 00:04:07,232
Second: the centre in Bihar founded by the Palas, known for tantric studies.

41
00:04:07,232 --> 00:04:08,538
Vikramashila.

42
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:18,295
Third: the centre in Gujarat known for a different Buddhist school and for secular subjects.

43
00:04:18,295 --> 00:04:18,929
Vallabhi.

44
00:04:18,929 --> 00:04:24,109
In Mains, a standard question asks about ancient Indian centres of learning.

45
00:04:26,900 --> 00:04:32,805
Distinguish Taxila's pattern from Nalanda's, name the endowments, and say what was actually taught.

46
00:04:36,067 --> 00:04:42,451
A seal takes its place in the gallery, the mark of an institution that outlived everybody in it — until it did not.

47
00:04:45,667 --> 00:04:51,364
Next gallery: the outsiders who came and wrote it all down, and what their accounts are worth.

48
00:04:51,364 --> 00:04:55,083
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

